<div class="page">
  <h1 style="font-size: 36pt; margin-bottom: 40px;">Eighteenth-Century Studies</h1>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">New in Paper</h2>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Feminism and the Politics of Travel after the Enlightenment</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, Queen's University</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">"Schick presents an intriguing examination of how notions of gender and perceptions of the value of travel interrelate in writing. The author treats selections from traditional, nonfictional travel writing as a solid critical framework in the analysis of how travel as a public, political, tough male activity...Recommended."</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 234 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-568-4 • $32.99 (E5.95) • Paper</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-568-7 • $79.99 (E49.95) • Cloth (2012)</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">New in Paper</h2>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Charlotte Lennox</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Correspondence and Miscellaneous Documents</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, California State University, Long Beach</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">"Schürer presents a learned, highly readable, and engaging set of all known letters relating to 18th-century English author Charlotte Lennox. His copious meticulous biographical research shows the literary life of Lennox, whose sex often negatively affected her ability to make a living. We describe Lennox's life, and contextualizes her prolific work...Recommended."</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">—Chronicle</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 480 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-567-7 • $32.99 (E5.95) • Paper</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-567-8 • $79.99 (E49.95) • Cloth (2012)</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">New</h2>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">The Idea of Disability in the Eighteenth Century</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, University of Winchester</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This ground breaking volume of essays explores the intersections between disability and the eighteenth century. It presents a new analysis of disability as an alternative to the Foucauldian, called variability, which is consciously historicist and centers on the disabled individual as "the same only different" from the non-disabled.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 280 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-559-2 • $80.00 (E49.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-560-8 • $79.99 (E49.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">New</h2>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Eighteenth-Century Poetry and the Rise of the Novel Reconsidered</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, University of Wisconsin, La Crosse, and Courtney Weiss Smith, Wesleyan University</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This volume explores the unexpected collisions between poetry and novels, offering a re-reading of literary culture and history. The novel poached from and featured poetry, and the poetry from and featured novels, and objects privileged by "rise of the novel" scholarship are only one part of a world full of imaginative things with indistinct boundaries.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 280 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-483-0 • $80.00 (E49.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-483-1 • $79.99 (E49.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Anna Letitia Barbauld</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">New Perspectives</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, Emeritus, Iowa State University, and Olivia Murphy, Murdoch University</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 20px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">James Arbuckle</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Selected Works</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, University of Bristol</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Holmes presents an annotated selection of the poetry and prose of James Arbuckle (c.1700-1742). Divided into three parts, includes the annotated texts of his two principal poems, <span data-field="poem_title">{{ poem_title }}</span>, grouped by themes; and a final section on his response to Swift's Irish satire, as demonstrated in selected essays from The Tribune and in A Panegyric.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">2014 • 214 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-553-0 • $70.00 (E44.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-553-1 • $69.99 (E44.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">New</h2>
      <h3 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Print Technology in Scotland and America, 1740-1800</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, Washington State University</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">McKay investigates the mediation of popular culture in Scotland and America, from the transatlantic religious revivals known as the Great Awakening to the rise of the printing press. The book examines the tension between unity and fragmentation that characterizes eighteenth-century Scottish and American culture.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 326 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-544-3 • $90.00 (E57.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-544-8 • $89.99 (E57.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">The Matrimonial Trap</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Eighteenth-Century Women Writers Redefine Marriage</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, Macon State College</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Mary Delany's phrase "the matrimonial trap" illuminates the apprehension with which genteel women viewed marriage. The written word became a means by which to escape the power that they otherwise lacked. Through her writing, they made the inevitable acceptable while resisting their circumstances. Rhetoric allowed these women to define their identities as individuals.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 216 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-526-4 • $80.00 (E49.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-526-7 • $79.99 (E49.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Coyness and Crime in Restoration Comedy</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Women's Desire, Deception, and Agency</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, Agnes Scott College</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">"In allowing female actors to appear on stage, the Restoration may have marked a watershed moment in English theatre history, but this did not mark a watershed moment in women's social attitudes toward women, as Thompson expertly demonstrates. Meticulously researched, clearly organized, and highly recommended."</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 202 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-532-6 • $34.99 (E21.95) • Paper</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-532-9 • $34.99 (E21.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Wordsworth, Hemans, and Politics, Romantic Crises</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, University of Denver</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Reading Christopher Smart in the Twenty-First Century</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span></p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, Plymouth University, and Noel Chevalier, University of Regina</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This collection of essays offers a renewed reading of Smart's poetry, literary, theological, and philosophical perspectives. The essays examine the enduring relevance of Smart's work, from Coleridge to a tasty contemporary, and the ways in which his poetry continues to be examined, and Smart's sacred poetry continues to resonate with the reader.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2014 • 274 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-586-9 • $85.00 (E51.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-586-2 • $84.99 (E51.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Enlightenment in Ruins</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">The Geographies of Oliver Goldsmith</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, University of Limerick</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">"Griffin offers a fresh look at the geography of Goldsmith, departing from the view that Goldsmith's world was a stable, and genders renders his canon as a complex, multifaceted, and often contradictory world. This critical stance allows readers to appreciate the tensions between Enlightenment and Jacobite politics."</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2013 • 226 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-506-9 • $85.00 (E51.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-506-6 • $84.99 (E51.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 20px;">Rococo Fiction in France, 1600-1715</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Choice Outstanding Academic Title 2013</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span>, University of North Carolina, Charlotte</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Excitable Imaginations</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Eroticism and Reading in Britain, 1660-1760</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">By <span data-field="author_name">{{ author_name }}</span></p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">"This provocative and exciting study explains the erotics of reading through the lens of eighteenth-century philosophy and proto-psychological theory. It examines how the reading experience to generate responses to the text, and how the reader's own desires and fantasies are shaped by the text and the cultural context."</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 10px;">Series: Transits: Literature, Thought &amp; Culture, 1650-1850</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">2013 • 286 pages</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-440-3 • $85.00 (E51.95) • Cloth</p>
        <p style="font-size: 10pt;">978-1-61148-440-8 • $84.99 (E51.95) • eBook</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Studies in Ephemera</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 0;">Text and Image in Eighteenth-Century Print</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Edited by <span data-field="editor_name">{{ editor_name }}</span>, CUNY Graduate Center, and Sally O'Driscoll, Fairfield University</p>
      <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
